        reading: [{ heading: "The absence, not the substance", body: "Augustine's answer to the problem of evil: evil is not a thing. It is an absence. Darkness is not a substance — it is the absence of light. Cold is not a substance — it is the absence of heat. Evil is not a substance — it is the absence of good. God did not create evil. Evil is what happens when the will, which was made for the highest good, turns toward a lesser good as if it were the highest. The corrupt official who chooses power over justice is not choosing 'evil' — he is choosing a real good (power) in the wrong way, at the wrong time, for the wrong reason. The evil is in the distortion, not in the thing chosen. This is elegant. It also has a hard edge: if evil is just 'less good,' then genocide is 'much less good.' Augustine's response: moral evil is not a lesser quantity of good — it is a distortion of the will. The distinction between 'less' and 'distorted' is the argument. Whether it holds is 1,600 years of debate." }],
