        parentBriefing: "This is intellectual history, not catechism. The Five Ways are the most misunderstood passage in Aquinas. They are not five independent proofs you check off. They are five starting points that converge on a single question: is the world self-explanatory, or does it require a ground beyond itself? Teach the question, not the conclusion.",
        hardEdges: ["Do not teach the Five Ways as 'five proofs for God.' That is a caricature. Teach them as five questions about whether reality is self-explanatory.", "The Ways are philosophically contested. Hume and Kant challenged them. Modern physics has reframed them. They are not settled — and studying unsettled arguments is the discipline."],
        reading: [{ heading: "The five questions", body: "Aquinas's Five Ways, from the Summa Theologiae: (1) Motion — things move, and nothing moves itself; there must be an unmoved mover. (2) Causation — everything is caused by something else; there must be a first cause. (3) Contingency — things exist but need not; there must be something that exists necessarily. (4) Gradation — things are more or less good, true, beautiful; there must be a maximum. (5) Teleology — things act for ends, even without awareness; there must be an intelligence directing them. These are not five proofs you check off. They are five questions: is motion self-explanatory? Is causation? Is existence? Is value? Is purpose? Each starts with something obvious (things move, things exist) and asks: what would have to be true for this to be possible? Whether the answers work is 750 years of debate. The questions are worth asking either way." }],
        bigIdea: "The Five Ways are not 'prove God exists.' They are 'is the world self-explanatory, or does it point beyond itself?' Five angles on one question.",
