        parentBriefing: "The parent theological humility note: the Five Ways are philosophically contested. Hume and Kant challenged the causal and cosmological arguments. Darwin challenged the Fifth Way (teleology). They are not settled. They are worth studying because they are among the most careful arguments ever made for the claim that reality points beyond itself. Whether they succeed is a live question. Teach the question, not the answer.",
        hardEdges: ["Don't present the Five Ways as 'proofs that God exists.' They are arguments that reality points beyond itself. The conclusion (that the ground is God) is a theological add-on, not the philosophical argument.", "Darwin challenged the Fifth Way specifically. The first three (motion, causation, contingency) are untouched by Darwin. Don't let the student think Darwin refuted all five."],
        reading: [{ heading: "The post-Darwinian parent", body: "Aquinas's Five Ways: five questions about whether reality is self-explanatory. The adult question: how do you teach these in a world where Darwin challenged the design argument (Fifth Way) and modern physics reframed motion and causation? The answer: honestly. The Fifth Way (teleology — things act for ends) was challenged by Darwin's natural selection, which explains apparent design without a designer. But the first three Ways (motion, causation, contingency) are about the existence of the universe, not its design. Darwin does not address why there is something rather than nothing. The Fifth Way is weakened; the first three are not. Whether that matters is the student's judgment. The parent's job: teach the arguments, teach the objections, let the child evaluate. The model can explain both sides. The judgment — does reality point beyond itself? — is the child's, not the model's, not yours." }],
