        dek: "Your family will go through hard things. Julian's claim — 'the worst is not the end' — is not optimism. It is the hardest kind of hope. How do you teach it?",
        objective: "The adult can distinguish hope from optimism, evaluate Julian's claim in the context of their own family's suffering, and identify how to teach hope without false comfort.",
        parentBriefing: "The parent theological humility note: Julian is a mystic, not a systematic theologian. Her argument is experiential. A secular parent can engage her claim — that the worst is not the end — as a phenomenological observation about resilience and meaning-making, without accepting the theological frame (God's love as the guarantee). The distinction between hope and optimism is portable. The theology is optional.",
        hardEdges: ["Don't use Julian's hope to dismiss your family's suffering. 'All shall be well' is a claim about the end, not about the middle. In the middle, things are not well, and saying they are is toxic.", "If your family is in crisis, Julian's claim may feel impossible. That is fair. Hope is not required — it is offered. The sitting is about understanding the offer, not accepting it on command."],
        reading: [{ heading: "Hope in the middle", body: "Julian of Norwich: 'All shall be well, and all shall be well, and all manner of thing shall be well.' She said this during the Black Death. She saw the whole world as a hazelnut — small, held, loved. Her claim is not 'everything is fine.' It is 'the worst is not the end.' The adult version: your family will go through hard things — illness, loss, conflict, failure. Julian's claim is that the hard thing is not the last word. Not that it will be fixed. Not that it will make sense. That it is not the end. The difference between optimism ('things will get better') and hope ('the worst is not final') is the difference between a prediction and a stance. Optimism is a prediction about outcomes. Hope is a stance toward reality — a refusal to let the worst be the last word, regardless of outcomes. The parenting question: how do you teach hope without false comfort? By being honest about the middle: things are hard, they may not get better, and the worst is still not the end. That is Julian's position. It is not easy. It is not optimism. It is the hardest kind of hope — the kind that survives the Black Death." }],
        bigIdea: "Hope is not a prediction that things will improve. It is a refusal to let the worst be the last word. That is what Julian teaches — and it is teachable with or without the theology.",
        tryThis: [{ title: "Find your hope", minutes: 10, steps: ["Name one hard thing your family is in the middle of right now.", "Optimism says: 'It will get better.' Hope says: 'The worst is not the end.' Which can you honestly offer?", "Julian's claim: the hard thing is not the last word. Can you hold that — or does it feel false right now?", "One conversation: tell your child honestly. Things are hard. They may not get better soon. And the worst is not the end. That is hope, not comfort."] }],
